== 3.8 Chapter 3 Practice Drill — 40 Questions

40 questions graded in the order the CBT engine is likely to meet them. Allow 44 minutes; the key and a full solution for every question follow.

#mcq(1, [Dialogue in a play is best understood as],
  [a transcript of ordinary speech],
  [the playwright's commentary],
  [an exchange of information],
  [a form of action in which characters try to move each other]
)

#mcq(2, [A speech delivered by a character who is alone on stage is],
  [a chorus],
  [a soliloquy],
  [a monologue],
  [an aside]
)

#mcq(3, [A long speech delivered to other characters on stage is],
  [a monologue],
  [an aside],
  [a dumb show],
  [a soliloquy]
)

#mcq(4, [An aside is distinguished from a soliloquy by],
  [its length],
  [its position in the act],
  [the presence of other characters who cannot hear it],
  [its use of verse]
)

#mcq(5, [A soliloquy most characteristically allows the audience to],
  [see the set changed],
  [learn the plot in advance],
  [watch a mind decide something in private],
  [hear the playwright's opinion]
)

#mcq(6, [A stage direction reading *She does not answer* most likely functions to],
  [indicate a pause in the action],
  [tell the actor to be quiet],
  [make a refusal visible],
  [mark the end of a scene]
)

#mcq(7, [A character who denies caring while repeatedly straightening a photograph is revealing],
  [the truth through business with an object],
  [nothing],
  [that the stage is untidy],
  [that he cannot see well]
)

#mcq(8, [Verbal irony occurs when],
  [a character speaks in prose],
  [the outcome reverses expectation],
  [the audience knows more than the character],
  [the speaker means the opposite of what is said]
)

#mcq(9, [A fire station burns down. The event is an example of],
  [situational irony],
  [foreshadowing],
  [dramatic irony],
  [verbal irony]
)

#mcq(10, [Dramatic irony requires that],
  [the ending is unhappy],
  [the play is a tragedy],
  [the audience knows something the character does not],
  [the character is lying]
)

#mcq(11, [The host toasts “the truest friend I have” while the audience has seen the guest poison the wine. The line is],
  [sarcasm],
  [verbal irony],
  [dramatic irony],
  [situational irony]
)

#mcq(12, [Foreshadowing differs from suspense in that foreshadowing],
  [is always spoken by the chorus],
  [plants the knowledge that suspense then uses],
  [resolves the plot],
  [occurs only in comedy]
)

#mcq(13, [A character's change of costume between acts most likely signals],
  [the passage of a single hour],
  [a change of actor],
  [a change of status or fortune],
  [a change of genre]
)

#mcq(14, [A prop that keeps returning in a play usually functions as],
  [a substitute for dialogue],
  [a mark of realism only],
  [a carrier of memory or argument],
  [a stage necessity]
)

#mcq(15, [Lighting in a play can establish, without a word,],
  [the date of composition],
  [the author's intentions],
  [the play's running time],
  [the shift from public to private space]
)

#mcq(16, [Song inside a play most often functions as],
  [commentary on the action or a marker of community],
  [a way to lengthen the scene],
  [an interval entertainment],
  [proof that the play is a musical]
)

#mcq(17, [A tableau is],
  [a change of scene],
  [a frozen arrangement of bodies that states an argument],
  [a silent speech],
  [a musical interlude]
)

#mcq(18, [A group outside the action who speak in judgement on it is],
  [a soliloquy],
  [a subplot],
  [an aside],
  [a chorus]
)

#mcq(19, [A narrator-commentator in a play differs from a chorus in that the narrator],
  [never speaks],
  [sings],
  [frames and interprets the story as a single voice],
  [belongs to the audience]
)

#mcq(20, [A play-within-a-play usually serves to],
  [fill the evening],
  [double the main action so that its meaning becomes visible],
  [introduce a new setting],
  [replace the exposition]
)

#mcq(21, [A dumb show is],
  [a silent staged action that plants the plot],
  [an improvised ending],
  [a comedy scene],
  [a play without a script]
)

#mcq(22, [The masque within a play typically interrupts the action with],
  [a comic subplot],
  [a soliloquy],
  [a legal argument],
  [spectacle, music and dance]
)

#mcq(23, [Direct address to the audience has the effect of],
  [making the audience a party to the action],
  [replacing the chorus],
  [preserving the fourth wall],
  [shortening the play]
)

#mcq(24, [Comic relief placed immediately before a catastrophe usually],
  [heightens the catastrophe by lowering the audience's guard],
  [changes the genre],
  [has no function],
  [weakens the tragedy]
)

#mcq(25, [The division of a play into acts chiefly controls],
  [pace and the question the audience carries between scenes],
  [the rhyme scheme],
  [the number of characters],
  [the costume budget]
)

#mcq(26, [Stichomythia is],
  [a choral ode],
  [short alternating lines that turn conflict into a duel],
  [a silent action],
  [a long speech]
)

#mcq(27, [A speech that breaks off with a dash most likely shows],
  [that the character is not allowed to finish],
  [the actor has forgotten the line],
  [a change of scene],
  [a comic interruption]
)

#mcq(28, [A character repeats another's word back at him in order to],
  [show agreement],
  [refuse the other's framing of the situation],
  [mark the act break],
  [fill a silence]
)

#mcq(29, [The most revealing stage direction in an exam question is usually the one that],
  [gives the time of day],
  [describes the weather],
  [commands silence or a refusal],
  [names a piece of furniture]
)

#mcq(30, [Costume is useful to a playwright because it],
  [can establish rank and period before a word is spoken],
  [is cheap],
  [identifies the playwright],
  [replaces make-up]
)

#mcq(31, [Which is NOT a frame device?],
  [play-within-a-play],
  [dumb show],
  [chorus],
  [stichomythia]
)

#mcq(32, [A question asking “what does the aside reveal?” is really asking],
  [who else is on stage],
  [what the speaker cannot say aloud to those present],
  [which act we are in],
  [how long the line is]
)

#mcq(33, [An audience that has been shown the murder weapon will experience the next scene as],
  [comedy],
  [melodrama],
  [farce],
  [suspense]
)

#mcq(34, [The chief difference between dramatic irony and suspense is that dramatic irony],
  [is a structure of knowledge, while suspense is the tension that structure produces],
  [is always comic],
  [requires a villain],
  [ends the play]
)

#mcq(35, [Music in a play can do all of the following EXCEPT],
  [establish mood],
  [replace the need for a plot],
  [comment on the action],
  [mark ritual]
)

#mcq(36, [“He says he will not go, and the stage direction reads *He puts on his coat*.” The direction produces],
  [a coincidence],
  [a contradiction between words and action],
  [a soliloquy],
  [a choral comment]
)

#mcq(37, [A silent scene staged before the main action, showing the same events in miniature, is],
  [a dumb show],
  [an aside],
  [a monologue],
  [a masque]
)

#mcq(38, [The effect of an aside depends chiefly on],
  [the length of the line],
  [the actor's volume],
  [the audience's awareness that others on stage cannot hear it],
  [the position in the act]
)

#mcq(39, [“The play's comic scenes are its weakest part.” A candidate arguing the opposite would point to],
  [their length],
  [the costumes used],
  [the number of characters in them],
  [their placement and the contrast they create]
)

#mcq(40, [Which technique gives the audience a judgment on a character without any other character making it?],
  [stichomythia],
  [comic relief],
  [the act break],
  [the soliloquy]
)

