  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Stage speech does something — warns, refuses, exposes. Information is a by-product, and the playwright's commentary comes through devices, not through the dialogue itself.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Solitude is the test. A monologue can be delivered with listeners present; an aside requires others who cannot hear it.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It is a monologue: one voice, but with an on-stage audience, and therefore a public argument rather than a private confidence.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Length is not the test. An aside happens among people, and its effect comes from the secrecy inside company.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Soliloquies show a decision being made, often with a reversal inside the speech — that is what makes them dramatic rather than confessional.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A negative direction is nearly always a visible refusal, and its meaning lies in what the character will not say.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Business can contradict speech, and the contradiction is the point: the hands tell the audience what the mouth denies.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Verbal irony is a gap in meaning inside the words. The reversal of outcome is situational and the audience's superior knowledge is dramatic.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The outcome reverses the expectation built into the institution, which is situational irony, and no speaker is involved.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The gap between the audience's knowledge and the character's is the whole device. Sincerity on the character's part makes it sharper, not weaker.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The host is sincere and the audience is not fooled: superior knowledge on the audience's side is dramatic irony.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Foreshadowing is the planting; suspense is the holding of attention once the audience has been told enough to worry.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Costume carries rank and self-image, so dressing a character twice tells the story of his rise or fall without a line.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Repetition turns an object into evidence: it accumulates meaning each time it is handled.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Light concentrates attention and can seal off a space, which is why it marks the move into privacy or secrecy.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Songs carry judgment, ritual and communal presence, especially in African drama, where they are part of the action.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The picture is held so the audience can read it: who stands, who kneels, who is excluded.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    That is the chorus, Greek in origin and central to much African drama, where it may also sing and dance.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The function is similar but the instrument is one voice, which can address the audience directly and shape how the story is received.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The inner play mirrors and accuses; the reaction of the watched character is the evidence it produces.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It is mute and it prefigures: the audience is shown what will happen before the words arrive.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The masque is courtly spectacle, and its intrusion usually sets an ordered or illusory world against the play's real one.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It breaks the illusion deliberately, admitting the house as a witness and often as an accomplice.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Relief is a heightening device: the audience relaxes, and the next blow lands harder.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A break is a decision about where to leave the audience, and what they are left holding is the question the next act answers.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The rapid turn-taking is the dramatic point: the exchange becomes combat at speed.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Interruption is a power move by whoever cuts the speaker off, and the unfinished sentence measures that power.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Repetition is contestation: the word is taken and returned with a different meaning attached.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Silence and refusal cannot be explained by the words, so they force an answer about what is being protected.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The audience reads dress instantly, so costume is exposition that costs no dialogue.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Stichomythia is a pattern of dialogue inside the action. The other three put something around the action to comment on it.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The aside exists to carry the unsayable, so its content is defined by the company it must be hidden from.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Knowledge creates anticipation: the audience waits for the moment the characters will find out.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    One is the arrangement of knowledge, the other the feeling it generates. They usually travel together but are not the same thing.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Music shapes response but cannot supply the action's causal chain. The other three are its standard functions.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The coat contradicts the refusal, and the audience reads the action rather than the speech.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Silent, prefigurative and miniature: that is the dumb show.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The secrecy inside company is the mechanism; the audience is admitted to a confidence the other characters are denied.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Function is argued from placement and contrast, which is why the answer must describe where the scene sits and what it sits beside.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The soliloquy grants the audience unmediated access to the speaker's mind, so they judge him on his own words, unedited.
  ]

]

